############## KEY — Kinetic-Ballooning-Mode Eigenvalue Yielder ##############

The Kinetic-Ballooning-Mode Eigenvalue Yielder (KEY) is a code written in 
Julia that extracts linear growth rates, frequencies and eigenfunctions from 
gyrokinetic- or fluid-type equations. It gives a partial description of 
plasma performance in stellarator and tokamak magnetic-confinement fusion 
reactors. KEY takes flux-tube geometry and kinetic profile information as 
input. Using an iterative refinement process, it yields eigenmode information 
for ion-temperature-gradient-driven and kinetic-ballooning-type 
microinstabilities.

############################### Getting started ################################

To start KEY:

Open Julia in the directory where "main_KEY.jl" is located.

Run the following command: 

	julia> include("main_KEY.jl") 

########################### Required Julia packages ############################

To use KEY, you'll need to install the following Julia packages:

CSV
DataFrames 
DelimitedFiles 
FastGaussQuadrature 
GLMakie 
Interpolations 
KissSmoothing 
LaTeXStrings 
LinearAlgebra 
ProgressBars 
SpecialFunctions 
Trapz 

######################### Importing flux-tube geometry #########################

To import a gist geometry file, run:

	julia> import_gist("path/to/gist_file.dat") 

To import an s-alpha tokamak geometry, run: 

	julia> import_salpha()

NOTE: for more info on these functions, see: 

	-> src/import_geo.jl

################### Calculating eigenvalues and eigenvectors ###################

To evaluate eigenvalue plane (gamma, omega_r) for a fixed set of input 
parameters, run: 

	julia> condition_M_op(beta)

To calculate the eigenfunction for a given eigenvalue input, run:

	julia> M_op_efn_array_plot_any_input(eigenvalue)

NOTE: for more info on these functions, see: 

	-> src/matrix_formalism.jl

############################### Parameter scans ################################

To perform an automatic beta-scan using gist geometry, run:

	julia> automatic_betascan_general_geometry_zoomer()

To perform an automatic k_y-scan using gist geometry, run:

	julia> automatic_kyscan_general_geometry_zoomer()

NOTE: analogous functions exist for s-alpha tokamak geometry, whereby the 
geometry is generated consistently throughout the scan.

NOTE: an input-parameters list and eigenvalue-eigenvector data can be 
automatically stored to the output directory using these functions. 

For details, see: 

	-> src/automatic_parameter_scan.jl

